\tag{exp}
\tag{status}
\meta{updated}{2026-11-25}
\meta{pr}{17}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Updated on 2026-11-25 ([log-PIWX](log-PIWX), [dec-RLMN](dec-RLMN)) after closing both main-path lemmas of \code{\verb~|double_eval~}, reorganising the tree for issue \issue{22} ([log-I6C0](log-I6C0)), and removing plan-less "default" aliases.}

\p{\strong{The proof for \code{\verb~|math::double_eval::expf~} is complete.} \code{\verb~|expfDoubleEval_correctlyRounded : ExpfDoubleEvalCorrectlyRounded~} in \code{\verb~|LibcMath/Kernels/Expf/Proof/DoubleEval.lean~} proves that \code{\verb~|math::double_eval::expf~} (x86-64 non-FMA, \code{\verb~|LIBC_MATH=0~}), extracted from LLVM libc at \code{\verb~|e1a5f92db023~}, is correctly rounded across all \code{\verb~|2³²~} binary32 inputs in all four rounding directions of C and reaches no operation that C++ leaves undefined. It uses zero \code{\verb~|sorry~}, zero \code{\verb~|native_decide~}, and depends solely on the three standard Lean axioms \code{\verb~|[propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]~}.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Inputs}\th{Bit patterns}\th{Share}\th{State}}
\tr{\td{Zeros, infinities, NaNs, magnitude at most 2⁻²⁶, overflow, underflow}\td{3 749 616 693}\td{87.30 \%}\td{proved, \code{\verb~|Expf/Proof/DoubleEval/ShortPaths/Ranges.lean~}}}
\tr{\td{Tiny and near-underflow main-path ranges}\td{25 256 675}\td{0.59 \%}\td{proved analytically, \code{\verb~|Expf/Proof/DoubleEval/MainPath/ProductError.lean~}}}
\tr{\td{Main polynomial ranges (2⁻²⁴ ≤ x < 88.72…, -103.97… < x < -2⁻²⁵)}\td{520 093 927}\td{12.11 \%}\td{proved via error bound + 278 619 Chebyshev SLZ TMD certificates (\code{\verb~|Expf/Proof/DoubleEval/MainPath/Tmd/~}) + 103 pointwise exceptions (\code{\verb~|PointwiseExceptions.lean~})}}
}

\p{\strong{How the main path is proved.} By numerical analysis and kernel-verified TMD certificates ([prob-48KJ](prob-48KJ), closed; [ins-NHOJ](ins-NHOJ)). The function-independent layers live in \code{\verb~|LibcMath/Analysis/{RangeReduction,Horner,RoundingTransfer,ChebTmd}.lean~} for reuse by future functions ([note-adding-a-function](note-adding-a-function)).}

\p{\strong{What the statement rests on.} The translation by \code{\verb~|ast2lean~} and the definitions of \code{\verb~|LibcMath/Libc/~}, which are tested against the compiled code and not proved; FloatLib's model of IEEE 754 arithmetic. \code{\verb~|errno~} and the exception flags are outside the statement: [prob-VR5Z](prob-VR5Z).}

\p{\strong{Other plans.} Each other plan has a statement in \code{\verb~|LibcMath/Kernels/Expf/Statement.lean~} and a top-level theorem in \code{\verb~|LibcMath/Kernels/Expf/Proof/~}. The short paths of \code{\verb~|double_eval_fma~} (87.30 \%), \code{\verb~|static_rounding~} (87.69 \%), and \code{\verb~|integer_eval~} (87.69 \%, through \code{\verb~|integerEval_eq_staticRounding_nearest~}) are proved. Their main polynomial ranges are the open lemmas \code{\verb~|expfDoubleEvalFma_mainRangesChecked~} and \code{\verb~|expfStaticRounding_mainRangesChecked~}; \code{\verb~|integer_eval~} uses the second. The theorems of \code{\verb~|float_eval_cr_rn~}, \code{\verb~|double_eval_sse42~} and \code{\verb~|double_eval_aarch64~} are open. \code{\verb~|float_eval~} is not correctly rounded; its open statement is that it reaches no undefined operation.}
